# TTY(1)

## NAME

tty - get terminal name

## SYNOPSIS

| **tty** [ **-s** ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Tty* prints the pathname of the user's terminal - the name in **/dev** of whatever is open on
the standard input.

**-s** prints nothing; the answer is the exit status alone, which is what a shell script wants.
**Note:** v7's page documented neither the option nor the status, although the program has
always had both.

## DIAGNOSTICS

`not a tty` if the standard input file is not a terminal.

## EXIT STATUS

0 if the standard input is a terminal, 1 if it is not.

## FILES

/dev
: searched for the name

## SEE ALSO

ttyname(3)

## BUGS

There is no way back from a device number to a name, so ttyname(3) reads **/dev** and compares
i-numbers. A terminal that is not in **/dev** has no name, and one that is there twice answers
with whichever entry comes first in the directory.
